            <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-920841f88f2a4173859c6c0eaeff8e7f" textAlignment="1">
              <bounds x="800" y="20" width="460" height="442"/>
              <content>Purpose – Show the evolution timeline of the architecture work products towards the enterprise's goals.

Concerns – Management of architecture projects · Developing architectures.

Scope – Shall: the status of the architecture work products; the architecture milestones that set this status. May: the architecture projects scheduling the milestones; the versions of the work products; the correspondence with milestones of rows C, L and P.

Modelling rules – Architecture Projects (Work Package) realise Architecture Work Products (Deliverable) and trigger Architecture Milestones (Implementation Event). The status reached at a milestone is the work product's `Architecture Status` property. Versions are Git tags (see A6).

Deviations from the NAF ArchiMate guide – Architecture Status: property; Architecture Version: Git; Architecture Work Product: Deliverable instead of Product; milestone → enterprise phase: triggering instead of association.

View location – Views / &lt;n Level> / [&lt;Scope>] / A Architecture Meta-Data / Ar &lt;Subject>.</content>
            </child>
            <documentation>Shows how the architecture work products evolve over time, through milestones and architecture projects.</documentation>
            <property key="NAF Viewpoint" value="Ar"/>
            <property key="NAF Grid Row" value="A – Architecture Meta-Data"/>
            <property key="NAF Grid Column" value="r – Roadmap"/>
            <property key="NAF Steps" value="AV, MP, AG"/>
            <property key="ArchiMate Viewpoint" value="Implementation and Migration"/>
            <property key="Reference" value="NAF v4.1 ch. 3 (Ar); NAF v4.1 ArchiMate Implementation Guide ch. 51"/>
          </element>
        </folder>
        <documentation>One guide view per NAF viewpoint of the template, named by its code. Build each enterprise view according to its guide.</documentation>
      </folder>
      <documentation>Views of the Reference Libraries.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <folder name="1 Enterprise" id="id-fb826f6e36c745bcb4e23c528f1ea491">
      <folder name="C Concepts" id="id-63257e69ace64bf680e1796a254a544a">
        <documentation>Enterprise-level views of grid row C Concepts. Name views &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
Viewpoints of this row: C1 Capability Taxonomy, C2 Enterprise Vision, C3 Capability Dependencies, C4 Standard Processes, C5 Effects, C7 Performance Criteria, C8 Planning Constraints, Cr Capability Roadmap.
To create a view: copy its guide from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides > C Concepts, then remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements; or use the jArchi script provided with the template. Set the view properties NAF Viewpoint and Architecture State (and Enterprise Phase for Transition and To-Be views).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <folder name="S Service Specifications" id="id-92b215b5901c45e986b6b11fbab1d7fe">
        <documentation>Enterprise-level views of grid row S Service Specifications. Name views &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
Viewpoints of this row: S1 Service Taxonomy, C1-S1 Service to Capability Mapping, S2 Service Structure, S3 Service Interfaces, S4 Service Functions, S5 Service States, S7 Service Interface Parameters, S8 Service Constraints, Sr Service Roadmap.
To create a view: copy its guide from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides > S Service Specifications, then remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements; or use the jArchi script provided with the template. Set the view properties NAF Viewpoint and Architecture State (and Enterprise Phase for Transition and To-Be views).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <folder name="L Logical Specifications" id="id-4aff28797e304902b2175ae8ae957ee9">
        <documentation>Enterprise-level views of grid row L Logical Specifications. Name views &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
Viewpoints of this row: L1 Logical Taxonomy, L2 Logical Structure, L2-L3 Logical Concept, L3 Logical Interactions, L4 Logical Activities, L5 Logical States, L7 Information Model, L8 Logical Constraints, Lr Logical Roadmap.
To create a view: copy its guide from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides > L Logical Specifications, then remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements; or use the jArchi script provided with the template. Set the view properties NAF Viewpoint and Architecture State (and Enterprise Phase for Transition and To-Be views).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <folder name="P Physical Resource Specifications" id="id-4bf329daada941589921a86c15ed9470">
        <documentation>Enterprise-level views of grid row P Physical Resource Specifications. Name views &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
Viewpoints of this row: P1 Resource Taxonomy, P2 Resource Structure, P3 Resource Connectivity, P4 Resource Functions, L4-P4 Activity to Function Mapping, P5 Resource States, P7 Data Model, P8 Resource Constraints, Pr Resource Roadmap.
To create a view: copy its guide from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides > P Physical Resource Specifications, then remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements; or use the jArchi script provided with the template. Set the view properties NAF Viewpoint and Architecture State (and Enterprise Phase for Transition and To-Be views).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <folder name="A Architecture Meta-Data" id="id-e7d9d6e7d3794640b8fd4cebd28fcaa0">
        <documentation>Enterprise-level views of grid row A Architecture Meta-Data. Name views &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
Viewpoints of this row: A1 Metadata Definitions, A2 Architecture Products, A3 Architecture Correspondence, A4 Architecture Methodology, A5 Architecture Status, A6 Architecture Versions, A7 Architecture Metadata, A8 Architecture Standards, Ar Architecture Roadmap.
To create a view: copy its guide from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides > A Architecture Meta-Data, then remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements; or use the jArchi script provided with the template. Set the view properties NAF Viewpoint and Architecture State (and Enterprise Phase for Transition and To-Be views).</documentation>
      </folder>
      <documentation>Enterprise level: architecture views of the whole enterprise, by NAF grid row.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <folder name="2 Domain" id="id-8d4e99c80df64278859cd26f8e789754">
      <documentation>Domain level: create one sub-folder per domain, each with the five grid-row folders (C Concepts ... A Architecture Meta-Data). Empty in the template.
Views follow the same rules as in 1 Enterprise (naming, creation from the guides, view properties); see the documentation of the grid-row folders of 1 Enterprise.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <folder name="3 Programme" id="id-f5b326a19c5a48968c8cc345c8584496">
      <documentation>Programme level: create one sub-folder per programme, each with the five grid-row folders. The programme carries the overall roadmap, shared transitions and shared components. Empty in the template.
Views follow the same rules as in 1 Enterprise (naming, creation from the guides, view properties); see the documentation of the grid-row folders of 1 Enterprise.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <folder name="4 Project" id="id-d0029f766c8f450d94b4aa1cd43ed55f">
      <documentation>Project level: create one sub-folder per project, each with the five grid-row folders. A project belonging to a programme sets the view property Programme. Empty in the template.
Views follow the same rules as in 1 Enterprise (naming, creation from the guides, view properties); see the documentation of the grid-row folders of 1 Enterprise.</documentation>
    </folder>
    <documentation>All views: Read Me, Reference Libraries (metamodels, NAF Viewpoint Guides), then the enterprise's views by architecture level and NAF grid row.</documentation>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ArchimateDiagramModel" name="0 Read Me" id="id-b6843976d1d64da3b66d833d8a5c3ee4">
      <child xsi:type="archimate:Note" id="id-3a3fa577037b42a69b7d83975d52a9ee" textAlignment="1">
        <bounds x="20" y="20" width="900" height="2350"/>
        <content>READ ME - ARCHITECTURE LANDSCAPE (template v0.6)

1. PURPOSE
This model is the architecture landscape of an enterprise. It is inspired by the NATO Architecture Framework (NAF) v4.1 for method and structure, and modelled in ArchiMate 3.2 with Archi. It is an original work, published under CC BY 4.0; NAF concepts remain the property of NATO, ArchiMate is a registered trademark of The Open Group. The template contains no example content.

2. STRUCTURE
The four zones of the NAF architecture landscape (NAF v4.1 ch. 2, 2.15 to 2.20):
- Workspace: Git working branches.
- Reference Libraries: zone of this model, in every root folder and in Views. ArchiMate Metamodel (one generic element per type, default meaning), NAF Metamodel (one element per specialisation), Viewpoints and Concerns, Reference Architectures, NAF Viewpoint Guides.
- Architecture Repositories: zone of this model. The architecture content of the enterprise; approved content is on the main branch.
- Registers: jArchi reports.
Element folders: one sub-folder per type (active structure / behaviour / passive structure), with sub-folders per specialisation for types carrying at least two.
View folders: Reference Libraries, then 1 Enterprise, 2 Domain, 3 Programme, 4 Project, each split by NAF grid row (C Concepts, S Service Specifications, L Logical Specifications, P Physical Resource Specifications, A Architecture Meta-Data).

3. HOW TO START
Two ways to start, depending on whether the instance will follow new releases:
- Instance kept up to date (recommended): open Architecture_Landscape_&lt;version>.archimate from the release, then File > Save As immediately under your own file name. Identifiers are kept, so later releases can be imported (section 8).
- Stand-alone model: File > New > New Model from Template with the .architemplate. Archi regenerates all identifiers: the model cannot be updated by import later.
Then:
1. Rename the model &quot;&lt;Organization> - Architecture Landscape&quot;; fill in the model properties Organization and Model Owner; keep Template Version.
2. Create one sub-folder per domain, programme or project under 2 Domain, 3 Programme or 4 Project, each with the five grid-row folders.
3. Create each view in the folder of its level and grid row, named &lt;code> &lt;Subject>: copy the guide view from Views > Reference Libraries > NAF Viewpoint Guides, remove the Note, the Viewpoint and the reference elements, or use the jArchi script provided with the template; set the view properties (section 5).

4. MODELLING RULES
- A specialisation is set only when it changes the meaning; without specialisation, an element has the default meaning of its type.
- States, modes, transitions, standards, profiles, protocols and readiness levels are element properties, not elements.
- A Concern is a Driver specialised Concern, associated with Stakeholders and Viewpoints; an assessment of the situation is an Assessment linked to the Driver, never a Driver.
- No relationship between the architecture content and the generic elements of the metamodels.
- Layout: active structure on the left, behaviour in the centre, passive structure on the right; Motivation on the right; Physical below Technology.
- Names, documentation and notes in English; element names singular, in Title Case; every element and folder documented.

5. PROPERTIES
Views:
- NAF Viewpoint: code of the viewpoint (C1, S4, L2-L3...). Views are named &lt;code> &lt;Subject>.
- Architecture State: As-Is, Transition or To-Be.
- Enterprise Phase: name of the Plateau aimed at (Transition and To-Be views).
- Programme: programme of a project view (optional).
- ArchiMate Viewpoint: ArchiMate viewpoint of reference (optional).
Elements and relationships:
- Lifecycle Status: Planned, In Implementation, Active, Phasing Out, Retired.
- Change Reference: initiative or change request of Planned, In Implementation or Phasing Out content.
- State (repeatable, optionally &quot;&lt;mode>: &lt;state>&quot;), Initial State, Mode (repeatable), State Transition (repeatable, &quot;&lt;from> -> &lt;to> [&lt;event>]&quot;, the event being the exact name of an Event element). Label showing all states: ${properties:, :State}. A repeatable property is one key repeated, one value per line.
- The Event named in a transition triggers the element when it is a behaviour (service, function), or is associated with it when it is an active resource.
- Attribute (repeatable, &quot;&lt;name>: &lt;type>&quot;): attributes of an information or data entity (Information Object, Data Object). Physical Characteristic (repeatable, &quot;&lt;name>: &lt;value>&quot;): physical characteristics of a data entity.
- Standard (repeatable), Standard Group, Profile, Protocol (repeatable), Readiness Level, Architecture Status (architecture work products). Protocol and Standard may also be set on a Flow relationship (resource interaction); Readiness Level is set on the Milestone that defines the level.

6. VERSION CONTROL
- main holds the approved architecture content; a baseline is a tag of main.
- Branches: baseline/&lt;topic> (documentation or reconciliation), change/&lt;CR-id> (incremental change), initiative/&lt;name> (architecture cycle); every merge into main goes through a pull request, with Archi Validator reporting no warning.
- Planned content: approval (merge, Planned / Phasing Out) -> implementation (In Implementation) -> go-live (Active / Retired, tag of main) -> clean-up of Retired content.
- Governance (decisions, contracts, compliance, waivers) is managed outside Archi.

7. ADAPTATIONS OF NAF
- Viewpoints S6, L6 and P6 (sequences) are out of scope.
- States, modes, transitions, standards, profiles, protocols, readiness levels, architecture status: properties instead of elements.
- Architecture versions and baselines: Git instead of elements.
- Strict ArchiMate: relationships replace elements where NAF reifies them (needline, operational flow, resource flow, element correspondence); every deviation is listed in the Deviations section of the guide concerned.

8. REFERENCE LIBRARIES MAINTENANCE
The Reference Libraries are not modified in an instance. To update them, on a dedicated branch:
- File > Import > Another Model, select Architecture_Landscape_&lt;version>.archimate of the new release (Archi cannot import an .architemplate);
- tick &quot;Update existing objects and folders&quot; only; leave &quot;Update model information and top-level folders&quot; unticked, otherwise the model name and properties of the instance are overwritten;
- objects are merged by identifier (synchronized or added, no duplicate); this works only for an instance created from an .archimate file of a release (section 3);
- set the model property Template Version to the new release by hand, run the Validator, then merge through a pull request.
</content>
      </child>
      <documentation>How to use the Architecture Landscape template.</documentation>
    </element>
  </folder>
